{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D11", "D12", "D36"],
      "pins": ["D11.2", "D12.2", "D36.2"],
      "nets": ["/NET0037"],
      "observed": "The anodes of the three status LEDs D11, D12 and D36 are all on net /NET0037, and no other pin is on that net. Nothing supplies the anode side, so the LEDs cannot light. Their cathodes are driven through R70, R35 and R57 from Module2 nLED/activity pins, which suggests the anodes should go to the 3.3 V rail.",
      "expected": "The common anode node of these LEDs should be tied to +3V3, as the other status-LED anodes are (for example D5 via R69, and D9 and D1 on +3V3).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"11\") (name \"/NET0037\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"D11\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D12\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"D36\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"1\") (name \"+3V3\") ... (node (ref \"D9\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\")) ..."}
      ],
      "impact": "The Ethernet link/activity and related status LEDs D11, D12 and D36 never light, so the board's network status indication is lost.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["D20", "R83"],
      "pins": ["D20.2", "D20.4", "R83.1", "R83.2"],
      "nets": ["Net-(D20-K2)", "/NET0209"],
      "observed": "The second LED of dual LED D20 has its cathode K2 (pin 2) on Net-(D20-K2), which connects through R83 to /NET0209. The LED's anode A2 (pin 4) is also on /NET0209. R83 therefore sits in parallel with the LED and shorts it, so the LED has no voltage across it and cannot conduct.",
      "expected": "The current-limit resistor R83 should go from the LED cathode to a drive or ground node, not back to the LED's own anode net. The LED should be driven between its anode and cathode through the resistor.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"8\") (name \"/NET0023\") ... (node (ref \"D20\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"A1\") ...)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"141\") (name \"NET0209\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"D20\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"A2\") ...) (node (ref \"J1\") (pin \"6\") ...) (node (ref \"J36\") (pin \"6\") ...) (node (ref \"R83\") (pin \"2\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Y\") ...))"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R83 Net-_D20-K2_ NET0209 130"}
      ],
      "impact": "The second indicator LED in D20 never lights, and the drive from U11 Y is loaded only through a resistor that is shorted across the LED.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J13", "R78", "J21", "Q10"],
      "pins": ["J13.3", "J13.4", "J21.1", "R78.1"],
      "nets": ["NET0167"],
      "observed": "Pins 3 and 4 of connector J13 are both on NET0167, which also carries J21 pin 1 and the Q10 gate resistor R78. The connector's other signal-like pins are separated, so pins 3 and 4 are shorted together.",
      "expected": "Distinct connector pins should carry distinct signals. Pins 3 and 4 should only be shorted if the connector pinout intentionally bridges them.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"126\") (name \"NET0167\") (class \"Default\") (node (ref \"J13\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"Pin_3\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"J13\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Pin_4\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"J21\") (pin \"1\") ...) (node (ref \"R78\") (pin \"1\") (pintype \"passive\")))"}
      ],
      "impact": "If pins 3 and 4 are meant to be separate, the Q10 gate drive is shared with an unintended connector pin, which could misroute a signal.",
      "confidence": 0.15
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The clearest anomaly is that the anodes of status LEDs D11, D12 and D36 form an isolated net (/NET0037) with no supply connection, so those LEDs cannot light. A second, lower-confidence concern is that R83 shorts LED D20's second channel across its own anode and cathode."
}

